{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg-2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Salt of the Last Light\n\nFor thirty-one years, Edwin Marrow climbed the iron stairs of Gull Rock Light at dusk, wound the clockwork, trimmed the wick, and watched the sea for ships that had stopped needing him. The automation came in his twenty-ninth year. The Board of Trade sent a letter, a crate of replacement lenses, and a polite young man with a clipboard who called the tower \"a legacy asset.\" Edwin kept climbing anyway.\n\nNobody argued with him. The island had two houses, a schoolteacher who left on the autumn boat, and a fisherman named Ruth Penhallow who still sold him bread on Tuesdays and never asked why he bothered.\n\nHe bothered because the light was not only for ships. It was a promise made in brass and glass, that someone was awake out here, that the dark had a witness. His father had kept the light before him, and his grandfather before that, and the logbooks in the cabinet went back to a hand so faded it looked like a stain.\n\nThirty-one years of entries, and most of them read the same: *Wind WSW, light good. Sea moderate. Nothing sighted.* Edwin had come to love the sameness. Some days the entry was the only sentence he wrote all day.\n\nIn the winter of his thirty-first year, a storm came up from the southwest faster than the glass predicted. Edwin lit the lamp at four, though it was not yet dark, because the sky had gone the color of slate and the gulls had vanished. By six the wind was screaming through the gallery rails. By seven the sea was breaking white over the lower shelf, and the foghorn, rusted and temperamental, gave a single strangled groan and fell silent.\n\nHe heard the flare before he saw it. A red point, low and wavering, somewhere south of the rocks. Then another, and a third.\n\nHe grabbed the storm lantern, a wool coat, and the handset that connected him to the mainland, which crackled and died in his hand. He went down the stairs anyway.\n\nThe boat was a small trawler, her engine dead, pitching toward the Teeth, the jagged reef that had claimed more hulls than any chart admitted. Edwin could not reach her. He could only do what he had always done. He climbed back to the gallery, raised the lantern in slow arcs above the rail, and shouted until his throat tore, knowing no one could hear him over the wind.\n\nBut the light turned. Its great lens caught the storm and threw it outward in a sweeping beam, and the men on the trawler, cold and terrified, saw the one thing they needed to steer by: a steady, unwavering point that meant land, meant safety, meant someone was there.\n\nThey fought the wheel toward it. The current shoved them, and a wave lifted the stern, and for one dreadful moment Edwin thought they would strike. Then the hull slid past the reef's last fang and into the calm water of the harbor mouth.\n\nRuth found him at dawn, sitting on the gallery steps with the lantern beside him, still unlit.\n\n\"They're all alive,\" she said. \"Three of them. Ben Carew's boy, and two from the mainland.\"\n\nEdwin nodded. He did not trust his voice.\n\nShe sat beside him, shoulder to shoulder against the cold. Below them, the sea had gone gentle and gray, the way it always did after it had tried to kill you and failed.\n\nThat afternoon, Edwin opened the logbook and wrote, in the same steady hand his grandfather had used:\n\n*Wind SW, gale. Sea heavy. Three sighted. Light held.*\n\nHe closed the book, climbed the stairs once more, and waited for dusk."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":963,"cache_read_input_tokens":4215,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":963,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_managemen